Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)
Geriatric & Frailty
Progressive exertional dyspnoea, Chronic productive cough, Widespread expiratory wheeze, Irreversible FEV1/FVC ratio <0.70

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-008
Reason for Visit: Acute worsening of breathlessness and increased sputum production over the past 48 hours.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute respiratory compromise characterized by increasing dyspnoea at rest and a change in sputum purulence. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to evaluate for an infective COPD exacerbation, prevent progression to acute hypercapnic respiratory failure, and avoid emergency hospitalization prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My breathing has become incredibly tight, and I am coughing up thick green phlegm that I can't seem to clear."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a known history of chronic, progressive dyspnoea that is characteristically worse upon physical exertion, alongside a history of significant and prolonged tobacco smoking. They report a chronic daily productive cough with white sputum as their baseline. Over the past 48 hours, the cough has worsened, and the sputum has changed from white to thick, purulent green. They have experienced increased dyspnoea, now occurring at rest, and report using accessory muscles of respiration to breathe comfortably. They deny high fevers or acute chest pain but note an increased frequency of their rescue inhaler use with diminishing relief.
Functional Status: Usually independent with slow pacing; currently struggling to complete basic tasks like dressing due to breathlessness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, increasing fatigue impairing ability to self-care or seek emergency help if sudden deterioration occurs.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.3 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 91% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Sitting forward, using pursed-lip breathing, speaking in short sentences.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals a barrel-shaped chest with an increased anteroposterior diameter. The chest is hyperresonant to percussion with significantly decreased breath sounds globally. Auscultation demonstrates a prolonged expiratory phase and widespread expiratory wheeze bilaterally, with scattered coarse crackles clearing partially with coughing. Cardiovascular exam shows normal heart sounds with no signs of profound right heart failure (cor pulmonale) such as severe peripheral oedema. (Irreversible FEV1/FVC ratio <0.70 is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding from historical spirometry).
Environmental Observations: Evidence of recent smoking in the home; inhalers are scattered on the table but lack a spacer device.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been over-relying on the short-acting bronchodilator without utilizing the prescribed preventer medication regularly.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) - Status: Acute Infective Exacerbation
Differentials:

Acute Pulmonary Oedema / Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of orthopnoea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, elevated jugular venous pressure, or fine bibasilar crackles; clinical picture is heavily obstructed rather than volume overloaded.

Pneumothorax - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical chest wall movement and bilateral presence of breath sounds (though diminished globally), without unilateral hyperresonance or sudden, sharp pleuritic pain.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual onset over 48 hours strongly associated with purulent sputum production, rather than sudden unexplained dyspnoea or hemoptysis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with the classic hallmarks of an acute infective COPD exacerbation: increased dyspnoea, increased sputum volume, and increased sputum purulence. Telephone triage correctly identified impending respiratory exhaustion. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period carried a severe risk of progression to acute hypercapnic respiratory failure, exhaustion, and the need for mechanical ventilation. This urgent after-hours intervention allowed for the immediate initiation of systemic corticosteroids and targeted oral antibiotics to halt the inflammatory cascade and treat the underlying infection, effectively preventing an emergency department admission and stabilizing the patient in their home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced a 5-day course of oral prednisone 37.5mg daily. Initiated a 5-day course of oral amoxicillin 500mg three times daily. Provided a spacer device and educated the patient on correct inhaler technique.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a structured inhaler routine and spacing of activities. Step 1: The patient strictly uses their preventer inhaler with the new spacer device every morning and evening while sitting upright. Step 2: Upon successful use of the spacer and preventer twice daily, the patient rewards themselves with a designated period of uninterrupted rest reading a preferred book. Cause-and-effect: Associating the consistent, correct use of the spacer device with a planned, enjoyable rest period establishes a sustainable habit, ensuring optimal medication delivery and preventing future acute exacerbations.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated currently.
DME/Safety (Optional): Spacer device provided directly during the consultation.
Follow-up: Routine GP review required within 3-5 days to monitor response to oral therapy and check baseline oxygen saturation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops severe shortness of breath at rest unresponsive to the rescue inhaler, confusion, severe chest pain, or a blue tinge to the lips or fingers.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical assessment indicates an exacerbation of the underlying lung condition; complete recovery to baseline may take several weeks.
Clinical Course: The steroids will begin reducing airway inflammation within 12-24 hours, and the antibiotics will gradually clear the infection over the five-day course. Breathlessness should steadily improve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you find yourself gasping for air even while resting, if your rescue inhaler completely stops working, or if you begin to feel unusually confused or drowsy. You must see your regular GP next week.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The chest infection has caused severe swelling inside your airways and increased thick mucus production. The steroid tablets will quickly reduce the swelling to open the air passages, while the antibiotics will kill the bacteria causing the green phlegm.
Actionable Steps: Take the steroid tablets and antibiotics strictly as prescribed. You must use the new spacer device with your inhalers to ensure the medicine reaches your lungs, rather than hitting the back of your throat. Reward your compliance with planned rest periods.

